    'version_history' => [
        ['version' => '2.51.0', 'date' => '2026-09-17', 'summary' => 'SaaS Product Finalization -- onboarding, payments, and the document identity system. THE PRICING DEFECT, TRACED TO ITS ACTUAL SOURCE: v2.50.0 changed PackageSeeder and every pricing surface, and production still rendered "Gratis / Rp490 / Rp1.490" for a whole release. A seeder is not the runtime source of truth -- the packages TABLE is, and db:seed had never re-run. Everything looked correct in review because it was correct; only the row was stale. Fixed in a migration, which every environment runs once on deploy, and a test now re-runs that migration against deliberately-stale placeholder rows so the gap cannot reopen. Verifying it in a browser then exposed worse drift in the same rows: max_ptw_users was NULL on every plan, and null means UNLIMITED to the entitlement layer, so every tier had been silently granting unlimited PTW Access seats -- an entitlement defect hiding behind a cosmetic one. Starter max_users was 10 against a 15-seat PTW allowance, an impossible combination since PTW seats are a subset of user accounts. The migration now standardizes the whole plan definition. LAUNCH PRICING: Starter Rp499.000/Rp4.990.000, Professional Rp999.000/Rp9.990.000, Enterprise Rp1.999.000/Rp19.990.000. Annual is presented as better value using the saving computed from each plan OWN two prices (17%), never an invented badge. SELF-SERVICE ONBOARDING replaces the mailto handoff, which opened the operating system "choose an app" dialog and did nothing at all on a machine with no mail client -- asking a prospect to compose an email in order to buy software is not an acquisition flow. A prospect now registers through a real form, confirms their email, reviews the plan and pays. TenantRegistration holds the prospect OUTSIDE the tenant isolation boundary: no Tenant, no Company and no User row exists until payment is confirmed server-side, so "a pending tenant must not have application access" is true by construction rather than by a middleware check that could be bypassed. The password is hashed at registration and the plaintext never reaches the database, so activation needs no credential email. TenantProvisioningService::activate() is the one activation path, idempotent under a row lock, and refuses anything not in the PAID state. MIDTRANS ADAPTER implements the EXISTING PaymentGatewayInterface -- Snap, so no card data ever touches IOMS. It verifies the SHA512 signature over order_id + status_code + gross_amount + serverKey BEFORE reading a single field. PaymentWebhookController is the only route that may mark a payment settled: it records the event first so the unique (gateway, event_id) index decides whether a delivery is the first, re-checks the notified amount against the invoice, and refuses to treat a fraud-challenged capture as paid. A browser reaching a success URL activates nothing -- the status page is read-only by construction. Recurring charging is never assumed: PAYMENT_RECURRING_ENABLED=false means invoice-per-cycle renewal, fully implemented, and the tenant subscription model is identical in both modes. DOCUMENT IDENTITY SYSTEM: each PDF used to reach into CompanySetting for whichever fields its own header wanted, so HSE grew a real letterhead and nothing else had one. DocumentEngine::identity() plus four shared partials (styles, letterhead, signatures, footer) now give every document the generating tenant own letterhead -- name, legal name, logo, address, NPWP, NIB -- and four new documents ride on it: Purchase Order/Surat Pesanan, Purchase Requisition/FPB, Goods Receipt/BAST and Work Order/SPK, all reusing the existing tenant-safe numbering. A signature box renders EMPTY when no name is known rather than inventing an approver. EXCEL gained a shared foundation (navy header, frozen pane, autofilter, A4 print setup) applied without touching how any export produces its data. PUBLIC SITE: Platform, Solutions, How It Works and FAQ were header anchors that silently did nothing from any page but the landing page; they are real pages now, and the mobile nav Pricing link was genuinely broken. HEADERS: 44 pages that hand-rolled their heading now use the shared PageHeader, which was itself brought into the Dashboard blue/navy hierarchy -- stronger steel wash, a real navy-to-brand identity rule visible at every breakpoint, and an optional icon chip using the established filled-chip treatment. TENANT BILLING at /subscription/billing shows plan, cycle, status, renewal, live capacity against real entitlements and full invoice history. MASTER ADMIN gained the onboarding pipeline, flagging the case that matters most -- a customer who has paid but has no workspace -- with a re-provision action that cannot activate an unpaid registration. ALSO FIXED, both found by actually running things rather than reading them: a shipped migration aborted php artisan migrate outright on MySQL 8.4 (information_schema returns ENGINE upper-case, and an auto-generated index name was 69 characters against a 64-character limit), so IOMS could not be installed on 8.4 at all; and the public /platform marketing route would have shadowed the Master Admin dashboard URL. Tests: 102 -> 129 (442 assertions). Three migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement or workflow logic weakened.'],
        ['version' => '2.50.0', 'date' => '2026-09-16', 'summary' => 'SaaS Finalization -- the public front door. THE DEFECT: every public "Get Started" CTA pointed at route(login), which is the one door a prospect without an IOMS account cannot walk through, and pricing existed only as an anchor inside the landing page so there was no URL to send anyone to. Sign-in and acquisition are different journeys and both need a visible door. Added /pricing and /get-started as real public routes sharing the exact PricingService payload the landing section and the authenticated Plans page already use -- one price source, three surfaces, so a price can never disagree with itself. Repointed every acquisition CTA in the shared public chrome (nav, mobile nav, footer, landing final CTA, plan CTA), and made the nav Pricing link a route rather than an anchor that silently did nothing from any non-landing page. The login page now signposts prospects to Get Started / View Plans. PRICING standardized to the recorded commercial model: Starter Rp1.499.000/Rp14.990.000, Professional Rp3.499.000/Rp34.990.000, Enterprise Rp7.499.000/Rp74.990.000. The seeder previously refused to touch prices because its own docblock forbade changing them without a documented business decision -- that decision now exists and is recorded in the file, so the guard was satisfied rather than ignored; the old 0/49/149 figures were USD-scale placeholders carrying an IDR label and would have rendered as "Rp49". Enterprise stopped being is_custom: it rendered as "Hubungi Kami" with no price, which contradicts Enterprise being the most complete STANDARDIZED tier rather than a negotiated custom build. IDR now formats as "Rp" rather than the ISO code. Annual is presented as better value using the saving computed from each plan OWN two prices (17%), never an invented badge, and a test asserts annual is genuinely below 12x monthly so the claim cannot silently become false. Plan capability lists are built from the plan real entitlement values (max_users, max_companies, granted workspaces) so the page can never advertise capacity the entitlement layer would not grant. ARCHITECTURE INSPECTED BEFORE BUILDING, and deliberately NOT rebuilt: PaymentGatewayInterface, a swappable PaymentServiceProvider binding, config/payment.php, PaymentTransaction, Invoice, Subscription with a real status model, and PaymentWebhookEvent::recordIfNew() which is already idempotent on (gateway, event_id) all already existed and are correct. NullPaymentGateway throws on every method rather than returning a fake success -- the right default. So no payment code was written: self-serve provisioning and the Midtrans adapter are documented in ROADMAP with the exact external configuration they require (merchant account, server/client keys, a reachable notification URL, and separately-activated Recurring), and /get-started says plainly that a subscription activates only once payment is confirmed. Nothing pretends to provision an account or take a payment. Tests: 95 -> 102 (323 assertions), including a Master Admin boundary assertion in BOTH directions -- a platform admin is redirected out of the tenant app, and a tenant administrator is forbidden from the platform surface. No migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement or workflow changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.49.0', 'date' => '2026-09-15', 'summary' => 'My Work brought into the shared card system, and the root cause of its drift removed. WHY IT WAS LEFT BEHIND: My Work (Field/Home) hand-rolled its tiles from the raw Card primitive plus an inline bg-brand-50/text-brand-600 chip. It never adopted ModuleCard, so none of the v2.43-v2.48 chip and tint work could reach it -- which is exactly why it still showed flat white cards with pale blue icons after every other surface had moved on. The fork itself had a legitimate reason, documented in the file: ModuleCard is a dense 32px enterprise tile, and a field user primary action needs a genuinely easy 48px touch target on a phone, not information density. So rather than either forcing the dense tile onto My Work (a real usability regression for the audience the page exists for) or restyling the fork in place (which would drift again at the next visual pass), ModuleCard gained a `size="lg"` row variant that preserves the 48px target, plus an `accent` prop that tints the surface with the icon own colour. My Work now uses the shared primitive, so future global visual changes reach it automatically. Its information architecture, tiles, order, labels, links and gating are untouched -- presentation only. ModuleCard also learned to accept an already-resolved URL as well as a Ziggy route name, because My Work receives fully-built URLs from the server. Tile accents are derived from the icon the server already sends, keyed in the page, so no controller, route or permission change was needed: incident red, checklist green, observation purple, isolation amber, permits and tasks IOMS blue -- semantics rather than decoration. SWEEP FOR THE SAME PATTERN: two Dashboard sub-components (SummaryStat in Today Alerts, and LeaderboardCard) also hand-rolled their own markup and kept the pale chip. SummaryStat was worse than cosmetic -- its chip was flat graphite regardless of accent, so an alert that mattered looked identical to a calm one; its chip now follows the alert own red/amber/blue accent. LeaderboardCard got the filled chip and the soft steel surface every other entry-point tile carries. No pale-chip signature remains anywhere in Pages or shared components. No iconography, business logic, permission, routing or workflow changes. Verified in the browser: My Work renders 7 tiles with solid 48px filled chips and rgb(255,255,255) glyphs across navy/green/purple accents on matching soft-tinted surfaces, no real horizontal scroll at 320/375/1440, Dashboard unchanged in layout. Tests: 95 passing (258 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.48.0', 'date' => '2026-09-14', 'summary' => 'Focused visual correction: the icon chip and the card surface are drawn from TWO different references, and v2.45.0 had wrongly softened both together. Compared the current implementation against 51c56d7 before changing anything. THE DIAGNOSIS: 51c56d7 chip = a solid accent-filled container with a WHITE glyph and soft depth; current chip = bg-success/15 with a coloured glyph, i.e. a pale tint. 51c56d7 wash = 6-8% (faint); current wash = 13-16% (the visible tint that was preferred). v2.45.0 pulled BOTH down when only the chip coloured GLOW was the actual problem, which produced tint-on-tint -- a pale chip sitting on a lightly tinted card, with nothing in the composition carrying contrast. THE CORRECTION: chip restored from 51c56d7 (solid gradient fill, white glyph, subtle depth) while the surface keeps its current visible wash. The chip now supplies the dark anchor and the surface supplies the soft colour around it, which is the intended relationship. Chip shadows return at ~0.38 alpha rather than 51c56d7 own 0.5-0.55 -- present as depth, short of the glow that was rightly rejected. Applied through the shared components so the relationship is consistent everywhere the pattern appears, not Dashboard-only: StatCard (21 pages, all six accents), ModuleCard (its chip had been flattened to pale steel in the same pass, and its surface now carries the soft steel wash too), KpiSummaryCard (its chip was a 10% tint of the category colour behind a glyph of the SAME colour -- the identical pale problem; now a solid fill of that category own admin-configured colour with a white glyph, and the surrounding surface picks up a soft wash of the same colour so each KPI card is tinted by its own category), and EmptyState (its chip was white-on-white). The two least visible washes (neutral and steel) were brought level with the semantic ones so an unaccented card is genuinely tinted rather than near-white. No layout, iconography, icon set, chart or decoration changes. Verified in the browser on Dashboard, HSE Overview and PTW: chips compute as solid fills with rgb(255,255,255) glyphs over the accent gradient, surfaces keep their soft accent tint, and there is no real horizontal scroll at 320/375/1440. Tests: 95 passing (258 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.47.0', 'date' => '2026-09-13', 'summary' => 'Completion pass for the global visual refresh. TINT AND ICONS: the accent wash moved from ~8-9% to ~14% at the corner easing through ~5%, so it is now clearly present as a coloured surface while staying a wash rather than a fill -- raised by COVERAGE, not saturation, and the hue is still always the accent own (green icon in a green card, amber in amber, purple in purple, red in red, steel when unaccented). Icon chips got a stronger tint behind a DARKER glyph: warning #F59E0B and success #16A34A are mid-tones that go muddy on a tinted chip, so glyphs drop to the 700 step -- defined, not washed out. The chip STYLE (soft tint, coloured glyph, hairline ring) is unchanged, as preferred. WHAT WAS STILL OLD, found by looking at rendered composition rather than at which components a page imports: every list page rendered its search + selects as a bare flex row floating directly on the page background, with a real surface above it (PageHeader) and a real surface below it (the results Card) but nothing holding the filters -- so a list page read as three unrelated fragments instead of one composed screen. That single pattern was the largest remaining marker of the older language. Added a shared FilterBar panel (layout only -- it owns no filter state, no query-string handling and no routing, so every page keeps its existing applyFilters logic) and promoted the filter row on ten list pages: PTW, Daily Reports, Employees, Incidents, KPI Records, Leave, Purchase Requisitions, Reports, Safety Observations and Work Orders. PTW COMPLETE REFRESH, not just its header: filters into the shared panel, permit numbers rendered as monospace navy identifiers so the number anchors each row, table select triggers aligned to the steel border family, the mobile card list given a real hover/press surface instead of a flat divided list, and pagination controls changed from bare bordered squares into proper raised controls. PTW status presentation fixed as a real semantic rather than a colour choice: `submitted` and `pending_approval` mean WAITING ON SOMEONE, and were rendering as a neutral outline chip -- a permit awaiting HSE approval looked the same as an informational label. Both are amber now, matching the same state on the issued PTW document so screen and paper agree. Settings -- the home of PTW Access, and one of the highest-traffic surfaces -- also still hand-rolled its heading on the bare background and now uses the shared panel. VERIFIED IN THE BROWSER: PTW list, detail and create form; PTW Access via Settings > Users; Login; landing page. Login button, PTW New Permit and the landing hero CTA all resolve to the harmonized rgb(33,109,196); all landing dark bands are the single navy rgb(15,39,71); no real horizontal scroll on PTW at 320/375/1440 or on Settings, Login and the landing page. Tests: 95 passing (258 assertions). No migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement, workflow or data-relationship changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.46.0', 'date' => '2026-09-12', 'summary' => 'PTW Access authorization fix + tinted-surface refinement. THE 403, traced route -> middleware -> role -> controller: the HSE sidebar offers "PTW Access" pointing at settings.index?tab=users. That page AND the write behind it (settings.users.ptw-access) are BOTH explicitly gated role:super_admin,hse in routes/web.php, and updatePtwAccess() additionally asserts canManageHse() -- HSE is authorized at every real layer. But config/departments.php mapped the `settings` prefix to `administration` ONLY, and RestrictDepartmentAccess took the FIRST matching department, so a department-scoped HSE user was denied at the ROUTING layer before any of those gates could run. The routing layer was contradicting the authorization layer, the same class of divergence as the v2.42.0 PTW fix. A prefix may now be listed under several departments and `settings` is listed for hse as well as administration. This grants NO new capability and the middleware still fails closed: every mutating settings sub-route (company/branding, modules, roles, companies, backup) stays in the separate role:super_admin group, and a user with department_key=hse but a non-HSE ROLE is still stopped by the route own role gate. Four of the seven new tests exist specifically to prove that -- an unrelated department still cannot open Settings, an HSE user still cannot POST settings.company, an HSE-department user holding a manager role is still blocked, and unrelated department boundaries still hold. VISUAL REFINEMENT, not a revert: the light restrained baseline is kept and only the inside of surfaces changed. v2.45.0 had pulled the StatCard washes down to 4-5%, which on a white card is below the threshold where the eye reads a tint at all, so the cards had gone back to looking flat. The wash now sits around 8-9% at the corner and fades through a mid stop, so it reads as a soft colour wash INSIDE the card rather than a corner blob or a solid coloured card -- and the hue is always the accent own, so a green icon sits in a green card, amber in amber, purple in purple, red in red. The icon treatment from the preferred baseline (soft tint chip, coloured glyph, hairline ring) is deliberately UNCHANGED. Depth stays neutral and restrained: the unused brand-coloured `glow` token was deleted outright, and the two remaining shadow-lift usages dropped to the ordinary card shadow. PAGES BROUGHT FORWARD: PTW Index still hand-rolled a 2xl/bold heading on the bare page background -- the older language, visibly behind every other module page -- and now uses the shared PageHeader panel, so its "New Permit" action sits where every other primary action does. Across the product, 43 pages that hand-rolled the page-title heading were recoloured from graphite to navy ink, matching PageHeader own spec, so page titles are consistent even where the panel is not yet adopted. Verified: PTW "New Permit" and the landing hero CTA both resolve to the harmonized rgb(33,109,196); all landing dark bands are the single navy rgb(15,39,71); PTW title renders navy inside the panel; no real horizontal scroll on Dashboard, PTW, HSE, Settings or the landing page at 320/375/1440. Tests: 88 -> 95 (258 assertions). No migrations. No tenant-isolation, RBAC, entitlement, workflow or data-relationship changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.45.0', 'date' => '2026-09-11', 'summary' => 'Global visual alignment -- soft, layered and atmospheric rather than colourful. This release deliberately PULLS BACK part of v2.44.0. That pass gave every StatCard chip a saturated gradient with its own coloured drop-glow; multiplied across a six-card row it reads as a strip of glowing lozenges, which is loud rather than premium. Chips are now a soft tint carrying a coloured glyph and a hairline ring, surface washes are weaker still, the coloured glows are gone, and the hover lift dropped from the strongest shadow token to the card token. Depth now comes from the tonal step against the ground and the shared shadow, not from saturation -- colour is left doing one job, marking semantics. Same correction applied to ModuleCard chips, the PageHeader accent rule and the Card navy tone. THE REAL SOURCE OF THE BRIGHT BLUE: v2.44.0 re-cut the `brand` SCALE toward the navy/steel axis but left the `--primary` CSS variable behind at the old 221 83% 53%, so every button, focus ring and primary control in the product still resolved to the original bright web blue while the surfaces around them had moved. That is why controls started reading louder than everything else. `--primary` and `--ring` now match brand-600 (212 71% 45%). Buttons were reworked so a primary action is the most SOLID thing on the page rather than the brightest: the weight comes from a navy-tinted shadow and a hairline inner top highlight, not from saturation. Deliberately NOT navy -- a navy button on a navy-anchored shell loses its affordance, and action hierarchy matters more than tonal uniformity. Outline/ghost/secondary moved into the same cool steel family so a secondary beside a primary reads as a quieter sibling rather than a different design system. LANDING PAGE, restructured not recoloured: the hero moved onto a deep navy atmospheric surface sharing the sign-in gateway DNA, and the eight-domain orbit was KEPT and re-lit rather than replaced -- it is the single clearest statement that IOMS is multi-domain and not an HSE tool. Three sections (HSE Workspace / People & Workforce / Data & Insight) were removed because they restated ground the Platform section already covers verbatim -- "HSE Management", "People", "Operations" and "Data & Insight" are literally its own categories -- so the page had five consecutive "here is another module" blocks that both repeated themselves and skewed the positioning toward HSE. 14 sections to 11, 9,362px to ~7,979px, with the two genuine differentiators (the PTW flagship workflow and the field experience) kept. The two dark anchor sections used graphite-900 (#0F172A) while the hero uses navy-900 (#0F2747); three dark bands in one scroll that are almost-but-not-quite the same colour reads as a mistake rather than a system, so all three are navy now with a soft steel or brand wash. Verified: login Sign in and PTW New Permit both resolve to the harmonized rgb(33,109,196); Dashboard stat chips render as 10% tints rather than gradients; the 8 real KPI sparklines still render; no real horizontal scroll on the landing page at 1440/375/320 or on Dashboard/PTW/HSE/Employees, tested by whether the document can ACTUALLY scroll rather than by comparing scrollWidth to clientWidth. No business logic, tenant isolation, RBAC, PTW authorization, entitlement, workflow or data-architecture changes. Tests: 88 passing (247 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.44.0', 'date' => '2026-09-10', 'summary' => 'Depth, material and real data visualisation across the product. THE BRIGHT BLUE EVOLVED: `brand` was Tailwind stock blue -- a bright, slightly violet web blue (#2563eb) that never belonged to the same family as navy (#0F2747) and steel (#3B82B6), so side by side it read as another product accent bolted onto an industrial shell. The mid/dark stops are re-cut along the navy<->steel axis (600 is now #2166C4): still confident enough to carry a primary CTA, no longer a generic SaaS blue. The 50-200 tints are untouched -- they are near-white washes where the hue barely reads. STATCARD, the densest repeated surface in IOMS (21 pages), went from a white rectangle with a coloured edge to a real information surface: each accent now tints the whole card with a soft diagonal wash of its own hue so a red card is legible as a problem at a glance, the flat pastel icon square became a lit gradient chip with its own glow, and the card can carry an optional inline trend. A row of six of these was the single largest contributor to the "flat and white" reading -- not because any one card was wrong, but because a row of them had no material. NEW Sparkline primitive -- and it is built so the obvious fake is impossible: it renders NOTHING below two real points, never generates, smooths, interpolates or pads a series, and draws a flat series as genuinely flat rather than stretching noise into a dramatic shape. So a surface only gains a trend where the caller already had real history. Wired honestly in exactly one place where real history already existed: the Dashboard KPI Summary row now shows each category own real 12-month series -- the SAME monthlyTrend payload the chart below it already consumes, matched by the category own short_label, tinted with the category own admin-configured colour. No new query, nothing invented, and a category with no series simply gets no line. Also: PageHeader gained a steel wash and a lit top edge so it reads as a surface catching light rather than an outline drawn on the page; Card gained an OPTIONAL closed set of tones (brand/steel/navy/success/warning/danger) so a page can mark a panel as carrying a semantic without inventing its own gradient -- passing nothing renders exactly what every existing caller already got; KpiSummaryCard picked up the same lift and steel treatment. New `lift` and `glow` shadow tokens are navy-tinted rather than neutral grey, so depth reads as part of the palette instead of dirt. QA: two further real overflows found and fixed in the topbar identity cluster -- v2.43.0 truncated the user name but not the role label beneath it, which still pushed the page past the viewport at 1280. Verified with a scroll-based test (does the document ACTUALLY scroll horizontally) rather than a scrollWidth comparison, which counts the scrollbar gutter and produces false positives. Dashboard, HSE Overview, Employees, PTW and Settings verified at 320/375/390/430/1280/1440. No business logic, tenant isolation, RBAC, workflow or data-architecture changes. Tests: 88 passing (247 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.43.0', 'date' => '2026-09-09', 'summary' => 'IOMS-wide visual system, propagated through SHARED COMPONENTS rather than page-by-page styling, so the improvement reaches every module consistently instead of creating one-off treatments. THE STRUCTURAL FIX: the workspace ground was graphite-50 (#f8fafc) with two ambient washes so faint they were effectively invisible, so a white card had almost no tonal step to sit on -- that, not the components themselves, is why the product read as "a collection of white boxes on a white page". Stepping the ground to a genuinely cool graphite-100 with steel washes is what lets every existing white surface read as RAISED, and it is the reason the rest of this pass could stay restrained rather than recolouring components individually. SURFACE HIERARCHY, now an explicit three-tier ladder rather than an accident: Dashboard hero (deep navy to IOMS blue, white text -- the company-wide command surface, deliberately UNCHANGED as the reference) > department Overview band (soft steel/blue, tier two) > module page header (white panel with a navy accent rule, tier three). PageHeader was a bare h1 on the page background across 35 module pages, which is what left ordinary pages looking older than the shell around them; it is now a real raised panel. Making it navy too would have flattened the ladder and turned the product blue everywhere, which was explicitly ruled out -- it earns weight from being a real surface with a navy identity mark, not from tint. Other shared upgrades, each cascading to every caller: SectionHeader became an uppercase tracked eyebrow with a navy rule and hairline (same section language the Dashboard already used) so a page of stacked sections finally has rhythm; EmptyState became a dashed steel panel with a ringed icon chip so "nothing here yet" reads as a designed state rather than a rendering gap (64 pages); Card moved to the steel border family with a nudged shadow so it reads raised on the new ground; Table gained a tinted header band, since a data table header previously differed from its body by weight alone -- the first thing lost when scanning; Badge went to 600 weight with an inset ring so status is legible when scanning a list rather than only up close (59 pages); ModuleCard gained a navy-to-brand gradient icon chip and a hover lift so domain entry points feel like doors, not list rows. Seven pages that hand-rolled their own "No X found." text instead of using the shared EmptyState were unified. QA FINDING FIXED: the overflow sweep caught the topbar user-menu cluster pushing the page 31px past the viewport at the 1280 breakpoint -- pre-existing (this pass only recoloured the header) but real; the identity block now shrinks and truncates. Verified across Dashboard, HSE Overview, Employees, Projects, PTW, Settings and Warehouse at 320/375/390/430/1280/1440 with no real horizontal overflow (the residual 8px is the scrollbar gutter -- confirmed the document cannot actually scroll horizontally). No business logic, tenant isolation, RBAC, workflow or data-architecture changes. Tests: 88 passing (247 assertions). No migrations.'],
        ['version' => '2.42.0', 'date' => '2026-09-08', 'summary' => 'Product/UX correction pass driven by real use of the deployed app, not another audit. PTW ACCESS 403 -- ROOT CAUSE: two authorization systems had silently diverged. User::canCreatePtw() is deliberately a UNION (canManageHse() OR an individually-granted ptw_access flag) precisely so a Field/Operations user in a NON-hse department can author permits without joining HSE -- but config/departments.php listed permits-to-work as an hse-OWNED route prefix, so RestrictDepartmentAccess 403d exactly that user at the ROUTING layer before the controller capability check could run. The prefix moved to UNIVERSAL_PREFIXES: this removes a routing-layer guess, NOT an authorization check -- the per-action gates are unchanged and a regression test pins that an UNGRANTED department user still cannot create a permit and that other HSE routes still block. DASHBOARD IA: the dashboard route used to silently return Field Home for any Department User, so an HSE-scoped account clicking "Dashboard" landed on a task-first page of HSE tiles (Create PTW / My PTW / Digital Checklist / Safety Observation) while the nav still called it Dashboard -- the one route meaning "IOMS-wide company picture" meant something different per account, and it made PTW look like the primary content of the company Dashboard. Dashboard now means Dashboard for everyone; Field Home was NOT deleted but moved to its own honest route (my-work) with its own sidebar entry, gated to Department Users. LOGIN: rebuilt as a two-panel Premium Enterprise Gateway -- a deep navy brand surface establishing what the product is, meeting a clean white working surface for credentials. Replaces five ambient decorations (blobs, pulse-glow, rotated empty square, masked grid) that carried no information on an all-white page. Every word on the navy panel is real brand copy or a live prop; no invented customer counts, uptime figures or testimonials. Collapses to a compact identity band on mobile so the form stays above the fold at 320px. VISUAL SYSTEM: the sidebar is now a deep navy rail. Earlier passes tinted it and the page behind it but both stayed in the same near-white family, so the app still read as one flat sheet. The earlier objection to a dark rail (a dark ground compresses the range available to separate active/hover/DISABLED, and disabled carries meaning here) is answered rather than ignored -- every state was re-pitched as an explicit ladder, disabled navy-500 < idle navy-300 < hover white/6 < active white/10 + white text + steel indicator, with active also carrying weight and a left bar so it never depends on hue alone. navy/steel ramps extended additively; no existing stop changed. PTW DOCUMENT: the printed permit read as a form print-out -- one hairline status chip on an all-white page and three bare underscore rules for signatures. Now an issued instrument: a navy identity band, a semantic status band whose left rule carries state as position and weight so it survives the monochrome prints these are usually issued as, bordered authorisation boxes recording who signed and when (an unapproved permit shows an empty box, never a pre-filled name), and a document-control strip making a printed copy traceable. Still A4, Dompdf-safe (table/background-colour only), verified to render. PTW WORK LOCATION: no schema change was needed -- permits_to_work already had a location column validated and rendered end to end. Project/Asset stays a real FK owned by the Management domain and location narrows the permit to an area within it; the field simply sat ABOVE Project and read as an alternative to it rather than a refinement, so it was moved beneath Project, relabeled Work Location / Area, and given a hint that it does not alter the project master. HSE MASTER DATA: "Equipment & Master Data" read as the company general equipment/inventory registry and so looked like Asset/Warehouse data misfiled under HSE. The content is genuinely HSE-owned (APAR/P3K/HT/safety cones, inspection-tracked under canManageHse()), and moving it would have put it behind canManageAssets() and cut HSE off from its own inspections -- so the fix is the label, renamed Safety Equipment & Compliance. Tests: 80 -> 88 (247 assertions). No migrations. No RBAC, tenant-isolation or entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.41.0', 'date' => '2026-09-07', 'summary' => 'Per-tenant document numbering + tenant branding assets. NUMBERING (the last known tenant-isolation gap): numbering_formats gained a tenant_id back in Milestone 3, but the COUNTER never did, so every tenant drew from one shared numbering_sequences row per module+period. Not a confidentiality breach, but each customer saw GAPS in its own document numbers wherever another customer consumed the counter, gap size weakly discloses other tenants volume, and in an HSE context a permit register with holes reads to an auditor like missing or destroyed records. Fixed with a tenant_scope column (the same always-NOT-NULL device ADR 025 established for company_scope, because every SQL engine treats each NULL in a unique index as distinct, so a nullable tenant_id in the key would not prevent duplicate rows). THE BACKFILL DELIBERATELY DOES NOT PARSE DOCUMENT NUMBERS: the earlier recommendation was to seed each tenant from MAX(issued number), which would mean reverse-engineering 29 modules editable prefix/pattern/padding formats -- fragile, and it fails once an admin customises a pattern. It is also unnecessary, because every tenant drew from the shared counter, so that counter is ALREADY >= every tenant own maximum. Seeding each tenant from the shared last_number is therefore deterministic and provably collision-free: tenant_next = shared + 1 exceeds anything that tenant holds, nothing is parsed, no counter resets to 0, and the number issued immediately after the migration is exactly the one that tenant would have received anyway -- no discontinuity at cutover. Verified on a real database: a shared counter at 42 with two tenants produced 43 for each with the platform row preserved. down() is equally careful -- it carries the MAXIMUM per-tenant value back onto the platform row before deleting tenant rows, so a rollback cannot lower the high-water mark and re-issue live numbers (verified: after a tenant advanced to 49, rollback left 49, not 42). Two tenants holding the same number string is correct and intended -- a document number is unique WITHIN a customer, like an invoice number; confirmed no module number column carries a global unique constraint. BRANDING: brand_wordmark_path and brand_icon_path have been READ since v1.5.3 with nothing able to write them; both are now on the EXISTING Settings > Company form rather than a second branding surface, and unblocked because company_settings became tenant-scoped in v2.40.0. Two real defects fixed while doing it -- ImageUploadField Remove button only cleared the PENDING file, so clicking it on a saved asset looked like it worked and silently changed nothing (a file field is simply absent when nothing is chosen, so the server could not tell no-upload from delete-this; explicit remove_* flags now carry that intent), and replaced assets were left on disk forever, leaking a file per upload. PTW: the form calls the field Penanggung Jawab Pekerjaan while every read-back surface -- the Show page, the Document view and the printed PDF -- called it PIC / Supervisor; the printed permit is the artifact an auditor actually holds, so all read surfaces now match the form. Technical identifiers (pic_employee_id, the pic relation) deliberately unchanged. Tests: 64 -> 80 (218 assertions). One migration. No functionality removed.'],
        ['version' => '2.40.0', 'date' => '2026-09-06', 'summary' => 'Full-system remediation, anchored on a P0 tenant-isolation defect in company_settings. THE ROOT CAUSE: the table was created in 2024 as a single-instance key/value store with a PLATFORM-WIDE unique(key), so it physically could not hold more than one row per key for all tenants combined; Milestone 2 added tenancy and nobody revisited it. Proven empirically, not inferred -- Tenant B READ Tenant A company name, logo, address, phone, email and brand colour, and Tenant B SAVING branding DESTROYED Tenant A because updateOrCreate([key]) matched the one shared row. It reached artifacts that LEAVE the system: DocumentEngine (every PDF letterhead), Excel export metadata, Report Center and Analytics. THE FIX: a two-tier model -- tenant_id NULL is the platform default (guests on login/landing, and any tenant without an override), tenant_id = X is that tenant override; get() resolves tenant -> platform -> caller default. A CompanySettingScope global scope fail-closes EVERY access path, not just the accessor, because five call sites deliberately bypassed it with raw Eloquent for a documented caching reason; those now use a new getUncached() that keeps the intended cache bypass without giving up tenant correctness. The cache is keyed per tier, since a bare "company_setting:{key}" key would have served one tenant a value belonging to another even after the query was scoped. MIGRATION BACKFILL is deterministic and never invented: exactly 1 tenant means ownership IS recoverable so the rows are assigned to it (fully closing the defect); 0 tenants means they are seeded platform defaults; 2+ tenants means ownership is genuinely ambiguous, so they stay as platform defaults -- preserving today exact behaviour and destroying nothing -- and a warning is emitted rather than guessing. Both branches were verified against a real database. ALSO FIXED: reports:dispatch-scheduled runs hourly with no HTTP request and therefore no resolved tenant, so TenantScope fail-closed and every scheduled report was generated EMPTY while its owner was notified it was "ready" (and titled IOMS rather than their own company) -- tenant context is now restored per schedule from the schedule own owner. Two request-input isolation gaps that survived the v2.37.0 sweep: kpi_category_id accepted another tenant taxonomy, and employee_ppe_id was a cross-tenant WRITE (storeReplacementRequest flipped the referenced row to replacement_requested and copied its owner company_id onto the new request); InCurrentTenant gained one-hop ownership resolution for tables like employee_ppe that carry no company_id of their own. Position department_id was likewise unguarded. Documentation corrected where it was actively misleading: ARCHITECTURE.md described only the pre-Milestone-2 per-company convention and never mentioned the actual isolation boundary; ROADMAP recommended building the branding UI on the broken storage model (now unblocked); ADR 025 records why numbering_sequences is still global-scope and why a naive fix would be worse. Tests: 44 -> 64 (171 assertions). No functionality removed.'],
        ['version' => '2.39.0', 'date' => '2026-09-05', 'summary' => 'Truthful zero states + repository consolidation. Browser-verified on a genuinely empty tenant: the Dashboard congratulated a brand-new customer with "Great job! No critical issues detected today," and HSE Overview rendered six KPI cards reading 0 in the healthy GREEN accent plus "Tidak ada yang terlambat saat ini" -- an all-clear asserted on a database with no employees and no records of any kind. For an HSE platform that is a safety claim nothing supports. Added TenantReadinessService (ONE memoised EXISTS query, no new table, no persisted onboarding state, no wizard -- derived live so it self-corrects), shared as a `readiness` Inertia prop for signed-in tenant users only. DashboardShell gained a single-line qualifying notice, which cascades to all nine department Overviews with zero per-controller wiring. StatCard now encodes "green is a claim": a positive semantic accent downgrades to neutral when no data could have produced it, while amber/red pass through untouched so a real warning is never silently muted. Propagated the honest pattern HSE Man-Hour already used ("--" plus an explicit unavailable reason) rather than inventing a second language for it. Also documented -- with a passing characterisation test -- a confirmed cross-tenant defect in company_settings (see ROADMAP "BLOCKED"); no schema change made. Brand integrity: the forbidden long-form product name removed from CompanySettingSeeder (a fresh install seeded it, and it then reached PDFs, Excel exports and the app shell), README, CHANGELOG and CLAUDE.md.'],
        ['version' => '2.38.0', 'date' => '2026-09-04', 'summary' => 'SaaS foundation (Master Audit P1). SecureDocumentController: authenticated, tenant-checked document delivery driven by a whitelist registry keyed by type + record id, with the path read from the database and never from the URL -- it streams from the new private disk OR the legacy public disk, so it ships with ZERO file migration. Subscription seat limits were previously only ever DISPLAYED, never enforced -- user creation now checks entitlement inside a locking transaction, reusing the exact shape already proven for the PTW quota. EmployeeSelector added as a shared platform primitive: seven controllers were preloading an entire employee directory into the page payload just to render a <select>, unusable at the 1,500-3,000 headcount IOMS targets; it is async, debounced, out-of-order-response guarded, department-grouped and id-hydrating, backed by a tenant-scoped EmployeeLookupController capped at 50 rows. Deliberately NOT built on Combobox (that one is free-text-over-preloaded-strings) and deliberately NOT solved by making pic_name free text, which would have destroyed the ability to answer "which permits was this person responsible for?" -- exactly what an HSE audit asks.'],
        ['version' => '2.37.0', 'date' => '2026-09-03', 'summary' => 'Security + testability (Master Audit P0). Three CONFIRMED cross-tenant defects fixed after auditing all 172 route-model-bound controller methods (163 were already correctly guarded): ProjectController::update never invoked its policy, allowing a cross-tenant WRITE; PpeController and TaskController allowed cross-tenant READS of employee PII, the latter handling a nullable tasks.company_id. Added the reusable InCurrentTenant validation rule, replacing unscoped `exists:` across 17 FormRequests so a submitted foreign id can no longer reference another tenant record; it memoises the tenant company set per rule instance so wildcard rules do not issue one lookup per row. Automated testing was impossible before this release and is now unblocked: phpunit.xml simply did not exist, and six migrations used MySQL-only syntax (MODIFY COLUMN, multi-table UPDATE ... JOIN, information_schema) that no other driver could run -- each is now driver-aware with the MySQL path preserved byte-identical, so production behaviour is unchanged and all 148 migrations provision on SQLite. Also fixed a real PTW timezone defect: the form seeded datetime-local inputs from an ISO/UTC string, the likely root cause of the reported "UI 4:37 PM vs PDF 08:37" mismatch.'],
        ['version' => '2.36.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Visual System 2.0: a genuine, visible transformation rather than another token-only pass. Added two new design tokens completing the directional palette (navy, steel) to the existing brand/graphite/semantic system -- no parallel color system. Dashboard\'s hero banner rebuilt from a barely-tinted white box into a real deep-navy-to-IOMS-blue "Command Center" surface (white text, an explicit "IOMS · Industrial Operations Platform" eyebrow, filter controls restyled for the dark surface). DashboardShell -- the one shared component every department Overview (HSE, Warehouse, Logistics, Procurement, Project Management, Assets, Maintenance, Quality Control) already composes through -- rebuilt from plain PageHeader into a soft steel/blue "domain summary surface," deliberately lighter than the Dashboard hero so Dashboard-vs-Overview stays visually distinct while both read as part of the same system; every existing caller gets this automatically, no page-level changes needed. Sidebar gained a subtle top-to-bottom tint (was flat white); TopBar gained a faint blue-tinted backdrop. PeriodFilter gained an optional triggerClassName so its selects can be restyled for a dark surface without duplicating the component. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, PTW workflow, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.35.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Visual System + Information Architecture Refinement: fixed a real reported mobile bottom-nav bug -- visibleNav\'s own "Dashboard" entry was duplicating the hardcoded "Home" tab (both pointing at the same route), and grouped items (e.g. HSE\'s "Permit & Work Safety") were skipped entirely, so PTW could never reach the bar while unrelated top-level items like Waste Management filled the slots instead. Rebuilt the candidate logic to dedupe Dashboard, surface each group\'s first real child (generic, not HSE-specific), and deprioritize Waste Management out of the primary slots per this pass\'s explicit direction -- Waste remains fully reachable via Overview/sidebar/More, only demoted from this one high-frequency bar. Renamed the pinned first slot "Home" -> "Dashboard" (there is no separate Home concept). AuthenticatedLayout\'s background gained a second, bottom-of-viewport tint layer addressing the repeated "too white" production finding -- the v2.29.0 tint only reached the top-left corner of a tall scrolled page. Most of this phase\'s scope (Dashboard vs Overview distinction, HSE Inventory, Digital Checklist, PTW Access, Waste language/workflow, landing page positioning, password visibility) was audited and confirmed already correctly addressed in prior phases (ae83d48 and earlier) -- not re-touched without a confirmed gap. No migrations, no RBAC/tenant-isolation/PTW-workflow/entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.34.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Post-Deployment Product Gap pass: audit-first review of six named production concerns found each was either already fully built (just undiscoverable) or genuinely absent. HSE Inventory (APAR/P3K/HT/safety cones) already existed in full under "HSE Master Data" -- relabeled to "Equipment & Master Data" with an explicit subtitle instead of being rebuilt. "Digital Checklist" (a Dashboard/Field Home quick-action label) and the sidebar\'s "Inspection" item were the same hse-inspections feature under two different names -- sidebar relabeled to match. PTW Access management existed only inside Settings > Users with zero sidebar entry point -- added one (same queryParams.tab pattern Administration items already use, no new authorization gate). Legal & Compliance confirmed genuinely absent (broad codebase search for license/regulatory/statutory/compliance found nothing) -- reported as missing, not fabricated. Waste Management\'s remaining English explanatory text (Dashboard/Form/Index/Show subtitles, an EmptyState, a CardDescription) naturalized to Indonesian per the established hybrid-language rule; Waste module/field names stayed English. Confirmed the "HSE-heavy Dashboard" concern in production actually describes Field/Home.jsx (a distinct, intentionally task-first page for the narrow is_department_user account type), not the actual Dashboard/Index.jsx (already cross-domain and already restructured in v2.29.0) -- documented, not conflated. No migrations, no RBAC/tenant-isolation/PTW-workflow/entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.33.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Phase 4 (Operational Documents + Waste + Backup/Security Audit): audit-first pass across Document/Waste/Backup/Security/Public Website. Real finding -- Waste Management\'s full generate-to-vendor-handover lifecycle (WasteRecord/WasteMovement/WasteMovementDocument) already existed with a real state machine; only a formal printable manifest was missing, added as waste-movements.pdf reusing the exact same DocumentEngine/PdfGeneratorService pipeline PTW/Material Request already use (no migration, no new PDF engine). Security audit found and fixed three real gaps: login/forgot-password/reset-password routes had no rate limiting (added Laravel\'s built-in throttle:6,1), WasteMovementController\'s document upload had no mimes: whitelist (added, matching every sibling upload validator), and .env.example still shipped the stale full product-name branding (corrected to "IOMS" so a fresh deployment starts correct). Extracted a new shared PasswordInput component and applied it to 7 password fields across Settings and Platform > Tenants that had no show/hide toggle (Login/Reset Password already had one since v2.27.0). Backup architecture audited and found to be a manual, on-demand, same-server-only mysqldump feature with no automation or off-site copy -- documented as a finding, not silently implemented as a false "solved." Public website positioning re-confirmed intact, not rebuilt. No migrations, no RBAC/tenant-isolation/PTW-workflow/entitlement changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.32.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Interior UI Completion Phase 3B (People, Operations, KPI): Employees Index and Man-Hour/Work Orders desktop tables switched their avatar/identity cells to the shared PersonChip component (brand-tinted, consistent with Employee Profile). Leave, Daily Reports, and Work Orders each gained a mobile identity-first card list (previously relied entirely on horizontal table scroll on narrow viewports), an unboxed filter toolbar, and consolidated desktop columns from 6-7 equal-weight columns down to 3-5 grouped cells -- no data dropped. Man-Hour\'s two summary boxes switched to StatCard (same already-real total_hours/record_count values). KPI Records gained real color-coded category quick-filter chips (each category\'s own admin-configured color, not a fabricated count). No target/trend/achievement percentage was added anywhere in KPI -- confirmed again that no such field exists in the data model. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, leave/workflow, or Man-Hour calculation changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.31.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Interior UI Transformation Phase 3 (People, Operations, Management/KPI, Settings, Micro-interactions): Employee Profile\'s identity block now sits on its own soft-blue tinted header band (brand-tinted avatar, name/code/status) separated from Work Information below, instead of one flat card. KPI Records gained a real primary summary card (total records, semantically accented when a category filter is active) before its table -- no fabricated target/trend, since no target field exists anywhere in the KPI data model (confirmed, not built around). Projects Index cards gained a status-accent bar (planned/ongoing/completed/cancelled), extending StatCard\'s own accent convention into Operations. Settings\' 16-tab wall regrouped into 5 visually separated clusters (Company, Master Data, People & Access, Workflow & Documents, System) -- same tabs, same RBAC gates, grouping only. Reusable micro-interactions added: Button gained real press feedback (motion-safe active:scale), CollapsibleSection\'s expand/collapse switched from an instant pop to the same smooth CSS-grid technique already proven on the sidebar\'s own sub-menus, Settings\' tab active-state gained a transition. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, or PTW/A4 document changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.30.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Interior UI Transformation Phase 2 (Domain Experience): actual domain-page redesigns, not another shared-component-only pass. Reports gained a real visual treatment -- PageHeader, KpiSummaryCard-based key summary (same icon/color-driven cards Dashboard already uses, same KpiCategory data, no fabrication), and department table headers switched from a solid color band to a tinted section-header style. Global Dashboard\'s HeroSummary now shifts its own surface tone (soft blue "all clear" vs soft amber "attention") instead of only a small footer strip. Procurement Dashboard\'s 8 stat cards regrouped into Pipeline / Delivery & Completion / Vendors & Value with eyebrow labels, communicating actual workflow position. Warehouse\'s "Barang Stok Menipis" card gets a real amber-tinted header when low-stock items exist. Logistics\' existing Material Flow pipeline visualization was deliberately preserved (already good, per this pass\'s own instruction) -- only its surrounding surface treatment changed, with the current bottleneck stage (computed from the same real per-stage counts already on screen) getting a stronger visual accent. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, or PTW/A4 document changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.29.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Authenticated UI Visual Transformation: shared-component-first pass, not a page-by-page audit. StatCard gained a real accent color-bar, hover lift, and an opt-in `hint` line -- cascades automatically into every domain dashboard already using it (HR, HSE, Logistics, Maintenance, PPE, Procurement, Project Management, Quality Control, Warehouses, Assets, Corrective Actions, Work Orders, the Global Dashboard). ModuleTabNav\'s active tab now gets a tinted surface instead of a bare underline, plus an optional real-data `meta` count line -- wired live into PPE\'s Employee/Replacement Due/Replacement Requests tabs from each page\'s own already-loaded paginator/array data, never fabricated. AuthenticatedLayout gained a fixed, very-low-opacity blue-tinted background layer (white Cards/Sidebar/TopBar still read as the brighter surface on top). Dashboard gained explicit "Company Snapshot" / "Needs Attention" section labels separating primary state from operational-attention KPIs. MobileBottomNav\'s active tab gained a top indicator bar + tinted icon pill. EmptyState\'s icon chip switched from plain neutral gray to the app\'s own soft-blue surface. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, database, or PTW/A4 document changes -- the A4 density work from 66addf6 is untouched.'],
        ['version' => '2.28.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'Product Experience Transformation, Deliverable B (PTW A4 Document): PTW Document.jsx and pdf/permit-to-work.blade.php redensified for a real A4 print target -- title/section/field/signature spacing roughly halved, Permit Type and PTW Number now share one compact row instead of stacking, keeping every section, field, and data point unchanged (numbered 01-05 information architecture from v2.20.0 untouched). Deliverable A (philosophy correction): the one Enterprise-as-"custom" line on the public Pricing section reworded to reflect IOMS as a standardized SaaS product -- Enterprise as the most complete tier (broader access, capacity, reporting), not bespoke development; is_custom flag, PricingService, and entitlement logic untouched. Broader interior-UI domains (Dashboard/HSE/Warehouse/etc.) re-audited and confirmed already consistent with the shared-component visual language established across v2.15.0-v2.27.0 -- no further change made where none was warranted.'],
        ['version' => '2.27.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Public Website & Auth Visual Transformation: redesigned Hero with a platform visualization (central IOMS hub + 8 real domain nodes, lg:-only for safe mobile fallback), a distinct English "Built for Industrial Operations" industry statement, and a white -> light-blue -> soft-blue ambient system with subtle motion-safe animation (2 new Tailwind keyframes, no new dependency). Redesigned pricing cards with visual hierarchy (no invented "Most Popular" claim) and plan framing text. Login gained a show/hide password toggle (also added to Reset Password) and the same visual identity as the public site. Public/authenticated app boundary, RBAC, business logic, and pricing data untouched.'],
        ['version' => '2.26.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Final Copy Consistency Check: naturalized the Dashboard hero subtitle, Material Requests subtitle, and four Warehouse/Item Master page subtitles to Indonesian. Actually inspected (not blindly modified) the PHP flash-message layer -- confirmed the existing ~176 messages are already uniformly clear, professional English; fixed the one genuine inconsistency found (5 messages with a redundant "...successfully." suffix). Logistics Dashboard and navigation labels re-confirmed already correct, left unchanged. Pure copy pass -- no layout, business-logic, or database change. This closes the IOMS UI/UX + copy consistency work.'],
        ['version' => '2.25.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Global UX & Copywriting Polish: naturalized 18 confirm() dialogs from English "Remove this X?" to consistent Indonesian "Hapus X ini?" across Daily Reports/Employees/HSE Inspection/Leave/Man-Hour/Milestones/PPE/Report Center/RFQ/Settings; added Indonesian explanatory copy to Login/Forgot Password/Reset Password (labels/buttons stay English); Settings Subscription tab copy naturalized; public website hero tagline corrected from a drifted "Industrial Operations Management Platform" back to "Industrial Operations Platform", subheadline naturalized to Indonesian. Pure copy pass -- no layout, component, or business-logic change. PHP-side controller flash messages (176 call sites) not systematically reviewed -- flagged as a follow-up, not silently skipped.'],
        ['version' => '2.24.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Complete Product UI/UX Transformation, closing the remaining gaps: Purchase Requisition (Purchasing) and KPI Records (Management) got the same identity-first table + mobile card treatment as prior passes; Reports gained a client-side "key summary" strip (no fabricated data) read before the detailed matrices; Logistics Dashboard and Report Center reviewed and found already good (Logistics already has a genuine pipeline visualization, not just cards). Presentation only -- no business logic, RBAC, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.23.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Complete Product UI/UX Transformation, continued: extended the identity-first list pattern (established on PTW/Incidents) to one representative page per remaining domain -- Safety Observation (HSE), Employees (People/HR), Man-Hour (Operations), Goods Receipt (Warehouse/Procurement) -- each gaining a mobile card list where none existed, consolidated desktop columns, and an unboxed filter bar. Public website hero/platform copy, Dashboard hierarchy, and Settings/Subscription were reviewed and found already adequate -- left unchanged. Presentation only -- no business logic, RBAC, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.22.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Complete Product UI/UX Transformation pass: new fixed mobile bottom navigation bar (Home + up to 3 authorized items + More, reusing the exact same RBAC/department-filtered nav array and drawer the sidebar already uses -- no second permission system); "IOMS" restored as the primary product name in the browser tab title, app config fallback, and public site footer (was drifting toward the full "Integrated Operations Management System" expansion, and this environment\'s own .env had a stale pre-rebrand "Shipyard Management System" title); Incidents list got the same identity-first table consolidation PTW Index introduced last pass. No business logic, RBAC, tenant isolation, SaaS entitlement, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.21.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Final Visual Polish pass (P0 scope): PTW Index desktop table consolidated from 9 equal-weight columns to 6 identity-first cells (Permit = number+type, Project/Location, Requester/PIC, each a two-line grouped unit) -- no data dropped, real visual hierarchy instead. Filter bar unboxed from its Card. Presentation only. Broader multi-module polish (Dashboard/HSE/PPE/etc.) intentionally left for a follow-up pass given this pass\'s own P0-first priority and proportionate scope.'],
        ['version' => '2.20.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Experience & Visual Polish pass: redesigned PTW Document/PDF around a numbered information architecture (01 Work Information -> 05 Supporting Documents) with a dominant document-title block and PIC/Workforce shown via a new reusable PersonChip avatar component instead of plain text; PTW Show page gained a more prominent identity/status header; PTW Create form gained numbered step badges. Presentation only -- no schema, workflow, RBAC, tenant isolation, SaaS entitlement, or quota changes. Same PdfGeneratorService/DocumentEngine pipeline, no new PDF engine.'],
        ['version' => '2.19.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Access Management Correction pass: fixed settings.users.ptw-access being gated role:super_admin only at the route level -- STRICTER than SettingsController::updatePtwAccess()\'s own canManageHse() check, meaning HSE could never actually reach it despite the controller already allowing it. Moved to its own role:super_admin,hse route group. Settings > Users tab now opens for HSE too, but split into two independent cards -- User Management (create/edit/delete/role changes) stays Super-Admin-only; Field & PTW Access (toggle + quota) opens to HSE. Requester/PIC/Workforce/quota logic re-audited and confirmed already correct, no changes needed. No HSE Dashboard, Field Home, PTW workflow, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.18.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Public Website / Landing Page Foundation: `/` is now a genuinely public route (previously redirected straight to /login for anyone not signed in) showing a real product website -- hero, platform capabilities, PTW-to-HSE workflow story, Field/HSE/People/Data sections, industries, FAQ, and a Pricing section driven live from Package data (never a hardcoded price). New PublicLayout (separate from AuthenticatedLayout, no sidebar). No customer names/logos/counts/testimonials invented anywhere. Authenticated users hitting `/` are still redirected into the app exactly as before. No RBAC, tenant isolation, PTW workflow, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.17.1', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Field Workflow Verification & Correction pass: fixed the max_users(10)/max_ptw_users(15) contradiction on Starter by raising max_users to 15, and PlatformController::validatePlan() now server-enforces max_ptw_users <= max_users for any future Plan edit; PTW Index (HSE workspace) now shows Project/PIC/Workforce headcount on desktop and mobile, via N+1-free eager loading. Re-audited Requester/PIC/Workforce/security -- all confirmed correct, no changes needed there. No HSE Dashboard, Field Home scope, PTW state machine, pricing, billing, or checkout changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.17.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'PTW Field Workflow Foundation + Controlled PTW Access: new per-user "PTW Access" grant (users.ptw_access) lets specific non-HSE Field/Operations users create PTWs, capped by a new package-level PTW user quota (packages.max_ptw_users, server-enforced with race-safe locking); Field Home\'s Create PTW tile now reflects this instead of HSE-role-only; PTW gained optional PIC/Supervisor and Workforce (real Employee references, tenant-scoped) shown consistently on Show/Document/PDF. Requester was already correctly server-derived -- confirmed, not changed. No HSE Dashboard, PTW state machine, RBAC, tenant isolation, or subscription state machine changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.16.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Global Mobile UX Hardening pass: fixed ModuleTabNav (PPE\'s tab bar) causing page-level horizontal scroll on mobile -- the root cause of a reported PPE screenshot regression -- by containing the scroll to the tab row itself; fixed two PPE list rows (Employees, Replacement Due) whose fixed-width columns exceeded narrow viewports; sidebar drawer now closes explicitly on mobile nav-item tap. Audited broadly (tabs, tables, dialogs, grids, fixed widths) -- most tables were already safely self-scrolling via the shared Table component\'s own wrapper, so no table changes were needed. No business logic, HSE Dashboard, Field Home, PTW workflow, RBAC, tenant isolation, subscription, or database changes.'],
        ['version' => '2.15.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'Product UI/UX Finalization: fixed the dialog component app-wide so tall forms no longer clip off-screen on mobile (max-h + internal scroll + safe margin, previously only one page had opted in); PageHeader now stacks title-above-actions on mobile instead of squeezing both onto one row; added a warning Badge variant so "Overdue"/"High priority" read distinctly from "Rejected"/"Critical"; Incident list converted to the proven PTW mobile-card pattern. Audit-first, shared-components-first pass -- no business logic, dashboard widgets, sidebar structure, or Field Home scope changed.'],
        ['version' => '2.14.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'SaaS Productization / Pricing Foundation: Package confirmed as the canonical Plan entity (currency/trial_days/is_public/is_custom added, no new plans table); new PricingService is the single source of formatted plan pricing (never hardcoded in a component); new tenant-facing Plans comparison page; trial-days-to-trial_ends_at wiring; upgrade/downgrade and billing-ready architecture documented for a later phase. No checkout, payment gateway, or final pricing decision -- explicitly out of scope.'],
        ['version' => '2.13.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'SaaS Phase 1 -- Subscription Architecture & Entitlement Enforcement: server-side per-workspace entitlement enforcement enabled by default (previously built, never wired on); a tenant with no grant rows is now safely treated as fully allowed instead of fully denied, so this cannot lock out a pre-existing tenant; new `tenants:sync-grants` command additively tops up a partially-granted tenant to its Package baseline; entitlement-denied messages now in Bahasa Indonesia. No new table, no payment/billing/pricing/checkout work (explicitly out of scope for this phase).'],
        ['version' => '2.12.0', 'date' => '2026-09-07', 'summary' => 'Product Finalization pass: fixed three real cross-tenant leaks found by a fresh security audit (Material Request full IDOR across 9 controller methods, PPE Replacement Request view/PDF IDOR, KpiRecordController default-state full leak), fixed a hardcoded-branding PDF footer, added two missing delete confirmations, improved the Employees empty state. No database change, no new route.'],
        ['version' => '2.11.0', 'date' => '2026-09-06', 'summary' => 'Field/Foreman Experience pass, Phase 3E-3H: Digital Checklist reworked into one-tap OK/Not OK/N/A cards (no more sideways scrolling), Safety Observation/JSA/HIRADC mobile grid fixes, Incident investigation Recommendations now visible read-only, a real "New LOTO" quick action added (previously had zero entry point). No database change, no new route.'],
        ['version' => '2.10.0', 'date' => '2026-09-05', 'summary' => 'PTW Document Polish pass (Phase 3D): fixed a real PDF/browser-document parity gap (rejection reason was missing from the PDF), HIRADC/JSA now show title/job_title not just the reference number, print output made dark-mode-safe with proper A4 page setup and break-inside protection. No database change, no new route.'],
        ['version' => '2.9.0', 'date' => '2026-09-04', 'summary' => 'Field/Foreman Experience pass (Phase 3C -- My PTW): new requester-scoped, card-based My PTW view (permits-to-work.mine) with real status filter counts and inline Resubmit for rejected permits; Field Home\'s My PTW tile now links here with live pending/active counts. No new table, no new role, no database change.'],
        ['version' => '2.8.0', 'date' => '2026-09-03', 'summary' => 'PTW Mobile / Task-First pass (Phase 3B): mobile card list for PTW Index (status always visible, no horizontal scroll), a real "resubmit" action for rejected permits (the state machine already allowed it, the UI never exposed it), rejection reason surfaced on the Show page, remaining mobile grid fixes.'],
        ['version' => '2.7.0', 'date' => '2026-09-02', 'summary' => 'Field/Foreman Experience pass (Phase 3A): the universal dashboard route now branches to a new task-first Field Home page for Department Users -- large action tiles (Create PTW/My PTW/Checklist/Observation/Incident/My Tasks), reusing existing routes and WorkCenterService counts, no new RBAC, no database change.'],
        ['version' => '2.6.0', 'date' => '2026-09-01', 'summary' => 'PTW Document View pass: new in-browser, printable PTW document presentation (company header, work info, hazards/HIRADC/JSA, gas test, authorization, signatures), reached via a new primary "View PTW Document" action on the PTW detail page; Download PDF unchanged, reusing the existing PdfGeneratorService.'],
        ['version' => '2.5.0', 'date' => '2026-08-31', 'summary' => 'Field HSE Experience pass (Phase 2): CAPA Open/Overdue/In Progress/Closed summary + Overdue filter, Global Search gained HIRADC/JSA, PPE Master vs Operations made visually distinct, mobile-responsive fixes across HSE Inspection/Incident/PPE, natural-Indonesian empty states with primary actions.'],
        ['version' => '2.4.0', 'date' => '2026-08-30', 'summary' => 'PTW UX + Field Operations pass (Phase 1): PTW PDF document generation, Create form progressive disclosure, Submit now reaches Pending Approval in one step, Reject requires a reason, mobile/responsive fixes across the module.'],
        ['version' => '2.3.0', 'date' => '2026-08-29', 'summary' => 'HSE Operations pass: new Waste Container Inventory (physical drum/IBC/jumbo-bag stock, separate from waste material records), Man-Hour 500 fix re-verified, PPE Master Edit audited and confirmed correct, Quick Actions gained a Waste entry.'],
        ['version' => '2.2.0', 'date' => '2026-08-25', 'summary' => 'IOMS OS Ecosystem pass: Man-Hour 500 root-caused and fixed (ambiguous SQL column), Quick Actions and Work Center Action Center extended across every department, Global Search extended (PPE/CAPA/PTW/Assets/Vendors) and its cross-tenant scoping gap fixed, a second cross-tenant leak fixed in Work Center approvals/PPE-alerts, sidebar navigation language reverted to English.'],
        ['version' => '2.1.0', 'date' => '2026-08-25', 'summary' => 'HSE Starter package made genuinely operational: Man-Hour opened to HSE (entitlement-dependency-rule fix), Package-to-Workspace/Module grant mapping wired into tenant creation, PPE Dashboard "+" action fix, optional per-workspace entitlement enforcement (off by default).'],
        ['version' => '2.0.0', 'date' => '2026-08-16', 'summary' => 'Milestone 2: Tenancy Foundation, Platform Super Admin, Package/Subscription structure, RBAC (spatie/laravel-permission), DB-driven Module and Workspace catalogs, Platform Super Admin console.'],
